ach side.\n\nFirst, let's isolate the $(x+4)^{3}$ term on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x+4)^{3} & =686 \\\\ (x+4)^{3} & =343\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take the $3$rd root of each side and solve for $x.$ Since $n=3$ is odd, there is only one real solution to this equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)^{3} & =343 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{(x+4)^{3}} & =\\sqrt[3]{343} \\\\ x+4 & =\\sqrt[3]{{7}^{3}} \\\\ x+4 & =7 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180401 #edb/ref 1061 30069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180401 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "52ee7c85-875f-5a5f-af14-c666ff0b02e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180401 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180402 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180401 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180402 #edb/ref 1061 30121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180402 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "247ebd22-9eec-58a6-b24f-de774c47432e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180402 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180403 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180403 #edb/ref 1061 30129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180403 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c7f88cee-5451-591c-bcf6-e4c817bdbf06" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180403 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180684 #edb/ref 1064 "How do we solve an equation with a variable raised to an odd exponent, such as\n\n$$\nx^3 = 125?\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation by taking the $3$rd root of each side and solving for $x.$ \n\nSince $n=3$ is odd, there will be only one real solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3} & =125 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{{x}^{3}} & =\\sqrt[3]{125} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{{5}^{3}} \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=5.$\n\nIn general, we have the following rule of thumb:\n\n*When $n$ is odd, and we take the $n$th root of both sides of $x^n = a,$ we get only one solution:* \n\n$$\nx = \\sqrt[n]{a}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180684 #edb/ref 1065 4834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180684 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a3deb3f5-8f54-5126-a222-4b9346b3c89f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180684 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562934 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186563767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562934 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186563804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562934 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186563811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562934 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562934 #edb/ref 1045 "Solving Equations With Odd Exponents Using the Nth Root Method" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562934 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186563811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562934 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562934 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562934 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "ab25c004-2d05-58f2-a0d6-54009af95a70" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562959 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562959 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562959 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "097ae540-a1c3-563f-958f-879f687d07c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563029 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563029 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563029 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "25486f62-97da-5109-9737-87f525d30db1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563033 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563033 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563033 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "27ed339f-1ba9-5371-b98d-4f3e5a4957d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563046 #edb/ref 1080 "-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563046 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563046 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2c8f8e6f-e697-5bb7-bf75-2e4331631d